;; A derived type holding a field whose type cannot have an `Eq`.
;;
;; A record field is almost always fine — the blanket covers any type with a
;; head constructor — so what is left is the structural types that have none. A
;; callback stored in a record is the one that turns up in practice.
;;
;; Every node a macro builds carries the *call site's* range, so the line a
;; derived comparison reports is the `type/derive` form rather than the field
;; that asked for it. The message names the type that has no implementation and
;; the implementation it was needed by, which between them say which field.

;; EXPECT-ERROR: no implementation of trait 'Eq'
;; EXPECT-ERROR: (-> int int)
;; EXPECT-ERROR: in the implementation of 'Eq' for
;; EXPECT-ERROR: Handler

(import (std prelude))

(type/derive (Eq)
  (: Handler (Record (: name string) (: run (-> int int)))))

(defun (main args) 0)
